  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nMainly dry conditions under mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies \nexist early this afternoon. While there is instability aloft (CAPE\nvalues around 1000 to 3000 J/kg), a low level cap is inhibiting \nconvection at the moment, and modeled atmospheric soundings \nsuggest this cap will persist through much of the day for most \nareas. However, over far eastern Montana (mainly Fallon and Carter\nCounties), surface temperatures warming into the low to mid 90s \nmay be enough for isolated thunderstorm development late this \nafternoon into this evening. With good instability and shear, any \nthunderstorm that develops in this region today may quickly become\nstrong to severe, with gusty winds, heavy rain, and hail being \nthe main threats. Currently, the Storm Prediction Center (SPC) is \nadvertising a Marginal Risk (Level 1 out of 5) for severe \nthunderstorms in this area. The other area to watch during the day\ntoday is the mountains, which may provide enough orographic lift \nfor convention to overcome the cap. Should this occur, high- based\nthunderstorms moving over the plains of south- central Montana \ncannot be ruled out. However, the chance remains low at this point\n(around a 15 percent chance). As you move west over western \nMontana, the chance does increase, and this is where the SPC has a\nSlight Risk (Level 2 out of 5) for severe thunderstorms later \ntoday.\n\nThis evening into tonight, a shortwave trough ahead of an \napproaching low pressure system is expected to pass through the \nregion. As this occurs, this shortwave is expected to provide \nenough lift to overcome the deepening low level inversion and \nsubsequent cap, allowing scattered showers and thunderstorms to \ndevelop and move north to northeastward through much of the area. \nWith this, the chance of precipitation is greatest over south- \ncentral Montana (where there is up to a 70 percent chance of \nprecipitation), but recent model runs suggest the chance of \nprecipitation has also increased over south-eastern Montana and \nnorth-eastern Wyoming (Sheridan County) where there is now a 25 to\n40 percent chance of precipitation. As previously mentioned, \nthere is instability aloft today, and this is expected to stick \naround into tonight. Therefore, a few strong to severe \nthunderstorms are possible late this evening into tonight, mainly \nover south- central Montana. With this, the main threats are heavy\nrain, gusty winds, and hail. Currently, the SPC has a Marginal \nRisk (Level 1 out of 5) of severe thunderstorms for this area as \nwell. By early Monday morning, expect the scattered showers and \nthunderstorms to decrease in coverage across the area. The risk of\nsevere thunderstorms will be much lower by this time.\n\nThe break in precipitation is not expected to last long before \nanother round of isolated showers and thunderstorms develop later \nMonday morning into Monday afternoon over south- central Montana. \nWhile this convection will be supported by the approaching low \npressure system, developing westerly winds will work to dry out \nthe lower atmosphere, potentially limiting convection. Any showers\nor thunderstorms that do develop will gradually move eastward \ninto eastern Montana by Monday evening. With this, cannot rule out\nlocalized strong winds under thunderstorms, but the risk of \nsevere thunderstorms remains low overall. The main hazard on \nMonday instead looks to be the synoptic winds associated with the \nlow pressure system. At this time, widespread wind gusts in the \n20s to 40s mph are forecast to develop Monday afternoon, with the \nstrongest gusts existing over the foothills west of Billings. \nAreas around Livingston to Big Timber and Harlowton may even see a\nfew 50 mph wind gusts late Monday afternoon into Monday evening \n(60 percent chance). \n\nTuesday, windy conditions look to continue across much of the \narea, though the strongest winds will shift to be over far eastern\nMontana. Again, wind gusts in the 20s to 40s mph are forecast to \nbe common, with a few 50 mph wind gusts possible around Baker, \nMontana (40 percent chance).\n\nWednesday into Thursday look to be mostly dry days as \ntemperatures warm back above normal behind the early week weather \nsystem. Friday into the weekend, there are signs that another \nweather system will bring precipitation and much cooler \ntemperatures to the area, however, uncertainty remains. LA",
